@* #685: the public Help/verifiability page. No [Authorize] — the verifiability
   story is for signed-out visitors too. Plain language, no CDS content. *@

<PageTitle>Help · Consultologist</PageTitle>

<section class="page-header">
    <div class="page-header__content">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Help</span>
        <h1 class="page-title">How Consultologist works — and how to check it.</h1>
        <p class="page-description">
            Consultologist drafts specialist consult notes and keeps every one traceable.
            Here's the flow, and the verifiability behind it — no jargon required.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="content-card">
    <div class="form-stack">
        <h2 class="panel-title">How a consult works</h2>
        <ol class="help-steps">
            <li>Enter the referral details and clinical context.</li>
            <li>Consultologist generates a structured draft consultation note.</li>
            <li>You review and edit — the physician stays in control before anything reaches the chart.</li>
            <li>Every run is saved in History with its full provenance.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="content-card" id="chart">
    <div class="form-stack">
        @* #188: the copy-back path, stated plainly. Present-tense, what works
           today; no competitor comparison (that lives on the marketing site). *@
        <h2 class="panel-title">Getting the note into your chart</h2>
        <p class="panel-description">
            When you're happy with a consult, <strong>Copy note</strong> puts the finished text on your
            clipboard as plain text — paste it straight into any EMR or document. There's no integration
            to set up and nothing to configure; it works with whatever chart you already use.
        </p>
        <p class="panel-description">
            Referrals can also come in by <strong>email</strong> rather than typed in, and where document
            OCR is enabled a scanned or faxed PDF is read the same way — so a faxed referral is answered,
            not rejected.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="content-card" id="verify">
    <div class="form-stack">
        <h2 class="panel-title">Verifiability: checking what you're reading</h2>
        <p class="panel-description">
            Every consult resolves to versioned, inspectable artifacts — the workflow that ran it,
            the rules it followed, and each step's provenance. When a run completes, its deliverables
            are fingerprinted (a SHA-256 hash) and recorded.
        </p>
        <p class="panel-description">
            The <strong>Verify</strong> button on a run recomputes those fingerprints in your browser,
            from the text the record carries, and checks they match what was recorded. A match confirms
            the letter you're reading is <strong>byte-for-byte what the engine produced</strong> — it
            hasn't been altered since.
        </p>
        <p class="panel-description">
            If the produced text was later deleted under the retention policy, the fingerprints are kept
            and still attest what was produced — but there's no text left to check them against, and
            Verify says so.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="content-card" id="glossary">
    <div class="form-stack">
        <h2 class="panel-title">Glossary</h2>
        <dl class="help-glossary">
            <dt>Provenance</dt>
            <dd>The record of how a consult was produced — the workflow, its versions, the inputs, and the fingerprints of every step. Kept for every run.</dd>

            <dt>Workflow package</dt>
            <dd>The versioned, immutable bundle that runs a consult: the steps, prompts, section standards, and output rules — published to a public registry so anyone can inspect the exact version that ran.</dd>

            <dt>Fork</dt>
            <dd>An account's own edited copy of a workflow package. The provenance shows when a run used a fork rather than the shared package.</dd>

            <dt>Catalog &amp; package format</dt>
            <dd>The output-contract catalog version the run used, and the manifest format its package was written against.</dd>

            <dt>Engine</dt>
            <dd>The exact engine build (a git commit) that ran the job.</dd>

            <dt>Contract</dt>
            <dd>The published provenance contract the record conforms to — what a record carries, and how its hashes are computed.</dd>

            <dt>SNOMED CT edition</dt>
            <dd>The clinical terminology edition the concepts were answered against, and the server build that served it.</dd>

            <dt>Pins &amp; attestation</dt>
            <dd>The workflow and its agents are pinned to exact versions and attested at startup; the provenance names the versions the deployed engine attests.</dd>

            <dt>Input hash</dt>
            <dd>A fingerprint (SHA-256) of the effective inputs a run used.</dd>

            <dt>Output (deliverable) hash</dt>
            <dd>A fingerprint of each produced deliverable — what Verify recomputes.</dd>

            <dt>Origin</dt>
            <dd>Where each input came from. A run labels each: read from a document, read from a transcript (the submitter's declaration), copied from a previous run's deliverable, filled from a form response, or replayed as a rerun. An edited value becomes typed text and carries no origin.</dd>

            <dt>Verify</dt>
            <dd>Recomputes a run's deliverable fingerprints in your browser and checks them against the record.</dd>

            <dt>Rerun</dt>
            <dd>Runs a completed consult again on the same inputs and package version; the verdict says whether every reproducible stage matched.</dd>
        </dl>
    </div>
</section>

